        <div id="furnace-watts" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Many Watts Does a Furnace Need? Usually 600 to 1,200 Running
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas or oil furnace does not burn electricity for heat, which is why a modest generator can keep a house warm through a winter outage. The fuel comes from the gas line or the oil tank. What needs electric power is the equipment around the burner: the blower motor that pushes warm air through the ducts, the draft inducer fan, the igniter, the control board and the thermostat. Together those usually draw somewhere between 600 and 1,200 watts once everything is running, with a short surge when the blower starts. Through a long outage, a clean <a href="/best-furnace-filter/">furnace filter</a> keeps airflow where the blower expects it; our filter guide explains MERV ratings and sizing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gas Forced-Air Furnace</h3>

          <p>
            The blower is the big load. Older furnaces use a PSC (permanent split capacitor) blower, typically rated between 1/3 and 3/4 horsepower, which draws roughly 400 to 900 watts running and two to three times that for a second or two at start. Newer high-efficiency furnaces use an ECM (electronically commutated) blower that ramps up gently and often runs at a fraction of the power. Add the inducer fan, usually 50 to 150 watts, and a hot surface igniter that pulls a few hundred watts for under a minute at the start of each cycle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil Furnace</h3>

          <p>
            An oil burner adds a burner motor and an ignition transformer on top of the blower. The burner motor is small, but it starts every cycle, so the total running load tends to sit a little higher than a gas furnace of the same size. Budget closer to the top of the range.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Boiler With a Circulator Pump</h3>

          <p>
            Hot water and steam systems have no blower. A gas boiler needs its controls, igniter and inducer, plus a circulator pump that moves water through the radiators. A standard wet-rotor circulator draws well under 100 watts, so a boiler is often the easiest heating system of all to run on a generator. Multi-zone systems with several pumps add up, so count every one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Read Your Own Nameplate</h3>

          <p>
            Open the furnace cabinet door (with the power off) and find the rating plate. It lists the blower motor's full load amps (FLA) and often the total unit amps. Multiply amps by 120 to get running watts. A furnace plate showing 8 amps means roughly 960 running watts. Then allow two to three times the blower's share for starting surge. Our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> lists typical figures if you cannot reach the plate.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Furnace Loads</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
