        <div id="clean-power" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Clean Power Matters: Furnace Electronics Want Under 3% THD
          </h2>

          <p>
            Wattage is only half of the question. The other half is the quality of the power, and this is where a lot of cheap generators and furnaces fall out. Modern furnaces are run by a circuit board, and many use variable-speed motors that depend on a clean, steady sine wave.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What THD Means</h3>

          <p>
            Total harmonic distortion (THD) measures how far a generator's voltage waveform strays from a smooth sine wave. Grid power in a typical home is low single digits. Inverter generators produce power electronically and are usually rated under 3% THD, which is the figure every pick in this guide either specifies or describes as clean power. Conventional open-frame generators vary widely, and budget models can be far higher. Our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/">inverter vs regular generator</a> guide explains the difference in more detail.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">ECM Blowers and Control Boards</h3>

          <p>
            An ECM blower contains its own electronics that read the incoming waveform. On distorted power, owners commonly report blowers that hunt, hum, run at the wrong speed or refuse to start, and furnace boards that throw error codes. A PSC blower is more tolerant, but the board that controls it is not. Clean power is the cheap insurance against a repair call in the middle of an outage, which is why we recommend an <a href="/best-inverter-generator/">inverter generator</a> for any furnace built in the last twenty years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Neutral, Ground and Flame Sensing</h3>

          <p>
            Furnace boards confirm the burner is lit by passing a tiny current through the flame to ground, a process called flame rectification. It only works if the power supply has a proper neutral-to-ground reference. When a furnace is plugged straight into a generator with a floating neutral, the board may light the burner, fail to sense the flame and shut it off seconds later. Connecting through a transfer switch or interlock that keeps the house panel's neutral bond usually solves this, which is one more reason to wire the furnace properly rather than with a cord.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polarity</h3>

          <p>
            Many furnace boards are also polarity sensitive: hot and neutral must be on the correct conductors or the board will flash a reversed-polarity code. A correctly wired inlet box and transfer switch keeps polarity right. Improvised adapters are where it goes wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Size Generator for a Furnace? 3,000 Watts Covers Most Homes
          </h2>

          <p>
            For the furnace alone, a quality 3,000-watt inverter handles almost every gas furnace and boiler. The question is what else you want running, and how the generator will connect to the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Furnace Only</h3>

          <p>
            A 2,500 to 3,000-watt inverter comfortably covers the blower's starting surge and the running load of a typical gas furnace or boiler. This is the right size if you plan to install a single-circuit furnace transfer switch and run everything else from extension cords or not at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Furnace Plus Refrigerator, Lights and Internet</h3>

          <p>
            Step up to 3,500 to 5,000 running watts. A refrigerator adds a few hundred watts running and a starting surge of its own, and you do not want the fridge compressor and the furnace blower starting at the same instant on a generator with no margin. We work through that exact load in <a href="/can-a-generator-power-a-furnace-refrigerator-and-sump-pump/">can a generator power a furnace, refrigerator and sump pump</a>. Our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generators for power outages</a> guide covers this middle band in depth, and the <a href="/best-dual-fuel-inverter-generator/">dual fuel inverter generators</a> roundup lists more options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Furnace on a 240V Inlet Box</h3>

          <p>
            If an electrician has installed a 30 or 50-amp inlet box feeding an interlock kit or manual transfer switch, you need a generator with an L14-30R or 14-50R outlet that supplies 120/240V. That opens up both legs of the panel, so the furnace runs regardless of which leg it sits on, and 240V loads like a well pump become possible. Expect 5,000 running watts or more.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Dual Fuel Suits Winter</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline degrades in storage and is hard to buy after a big storm. Propane keeps for years in a tank and you can store several outside. Five of our seven picks run on both. The comparison below shows how they line up.
          </p>

