        <div id="how-to-connect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How to Connect a Generator to a Furnace: Inlet, Then a Switch or Interlock
          </h2>

          <p>
            Furnaces are hardwired, so you cannot simply plug one into a generator. There are three safe ways to do it, all of which keep the generator isolated from the utility lines. Each should be installed by a licensed electrician and permitted where your local code requires it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 1: A Single-Circuit Furnace Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            The simplest setup is a small transfer switch mounted at the furnace, wired between the furnace's service switch and the furnace. In the normal position the furnace runs on utility power. Flip it, plug in a cord from the generator, and the furnace runs from the generator with the utility side disconnected. It suits a 120V inverter and costs far less than a panel-wide solution.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 2: Inlet Box and Interlock Kit</h3>

          <p>
            An outdoor <a href="/best-generator-inlet-box/">generator inlet box</a> feeds a breaker in your main panel, and an interlock plate physically prevents the main breaker and the generator breaker from being on at the same time. You then switch on the circuits you want, including the furnace, within the generator's capacity. Our <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> guide compares the two approaches.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Option 3: Manual Transfer Switch</h3>

          <p>
            A <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">manual transfer switch</a> mounts beside the panel and moves a set of pre-chosen circuits, typically six to ten, from utility to generator. The furnace gets its own switch position. It costs more than an interlock but makes it nearly impossible to overload the generator by accident, because only the circuits you pre-selected can run.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Never Do This</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Do not backfeed through a dryer or wall outlet.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A double-male "suicide cord" pushes generator power backwards through the panel and out onto the utility lines, where it can electrocute a line worker, and it energizes the exposed prongs of the cord itself. It also leaves your furnace connected without a neutral bond you can trust. Use an inlet and a transfer switch or interlock, every time.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="carbon-monoxide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Carbon Monoxide: Where the Generator Goes Matters More Than Which One
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter outages are when generator carbon monoxide deaths peak, because people run them in garages and close to the house to keep them out of the snow. Every pick in this guide has an automatic CO shutdown, but that protects the area around the generator, not the inside of your house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Twenty Feet Away, Exhaust Pointed Out</h3>

          <p>
            Run the generator outdoors at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust facing away from doors, windows and vents, as the CDC advises. Never run it in a garage, even with the door open, or in a basement, crawl space or enclosed porch. Our guide to <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers snow, rain and cord routing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the Furnace's Own Air Intake</h3>

          <p>
            High-efficiency furnaces draw combustion air through a PVC pipe that often exits through a sidewall. If the generator sits near that intake, the furnace can pull exhaust straight into its burner and the house's air. Locate both furnace pipes before you pick a spot, and keep the generator well clear of them. Keep the furnace's own exhaust pipe clear of snow drifts, too.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon Monoxide Alarms on Every Level</h3>

          <p>
            Install battery or battery-backup CO alarms on every floor and outside sleeping areas, and test them before winter. A plug-in alarm without a battery is useless in an outage. See our <a href="/best-carbon-monoxide-detector/">carbon monoxide detector</a> picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Gas, Propane or Dual Fuel for a Winter Outage?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel choice decides how long your heat lasts and how pleasant the outage is to manage. In winter, it tilts toward propane.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gasoline</h3>

          <p>
            Gasoline delivers full rated power and is easy to find in normal times, but it begins to degrade within weeks and can gum a carburetor in storage. After an ice storm, stations without power cannot pump it. If you rely on gasoline, store it in approved cans with a fuel stabilizer and rotate it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Propane</h3>

          <p>
            Propane stores indefinitely in a tank and burns cleaner, which is easier on the engine. Generators produce somewhat less power on propane, as every dual fuel listing here shows. In deep cold, a propane cylinder can struggle to vaporize fast enough under heavy load, so keep tanks out of the wind and do not let them sit in snow. Several full 20-pound tanks are a realistic multi-day reserve for a furnace circuit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cold-Weather Starting</h3>

          <p>
            Use the oil grade the manual specifies for cold temperatures, and start the generator monthly through the winter. Electric start helps, but its battery needs a trickle charge between outages. A generator stored in an unheated shed should be test-run before the first storm, not during it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="runtime" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long Will a Generator Run a Furnace? Plan Fuel by the Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            A furnace cycles on and off with the thermostat, so its average draw is lower than its running watts. That is good news for fuel. An inverter throttles down between cycles, which is a large part of why inverters stretch fuel so much further than conventional generators on this kind of load.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Simple Run Time Math</h3>

          <p>
            Look at the listing's run time at 25% load. A furnace blower plus a refrigerator usually lands around that level on a 3,000 to 4,000-watt inverter. The Honda EU3000iS is rated for up to 19.6 hours on 3.4 gallons, and the Champion lists up to 21 hours on propane. Plan on roughly one fill or one tank per day for a furnace-and-fridge setup on a mid-size inverter, more in a hard freeze when the blower barely stops.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much Fuel to Store</h3>

          <p>
            Most outages last less than a day, but winter storms can run three to five. Three days of fuel for a mid-size inverter is a sensible minimum: about 10 to 15 gallons of stabilized gasoline, or three to five 20-pound propane tanks. See <a href="/how-long-can-a-generator-run-continuously/">how long a generator can run continuously</a> for oil-change intervals during a long run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lower the Thermostat</h3>

          <p>
            Setting the thermostat to 62 to 65°F during an outage shortens every blower cycle, saves gas at the furnace and fuel at the generator, and keeps pipes safe. Close off rooms you are not using.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="troubleshooting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Furnace Won't Run on the Generator? Four Common Causes
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a furnace that works fine on the grid misbehaves on a generator, the cause is almost always one of these four, in roughly this order of likelihood.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Burner Lights, Then Shuts Off</h3>

          <p>
            This is the classic flame-sensing failure. The board cannot confirm the flame because the supply lacks a neutral-to-ground reference. Feeding the furnace through a transfer switch or interlock connected to the bonded main panel normally fixes it. Ask an electrician rather than improvising a bonding plug.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Reversed-Polarity Code</h3>

          <p>
            Hot and neutral are swapped somewhere between the generator and the furnace, usually in an adapter or a miswired cord end. Have the connection checked; do not keep resetting the board.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Blower Hums or Surges</h3>

          <p>
            This points to distorted power or an undersized generator. A conventional generator with high THD is the usual culprit with ECM motors. An inverter rated under 3% THD and sized above the blower's starting surge cures it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Generator's Breaker or GFCI Trips</h3>

          <p>
            Either the combined load is too high, especially when the fridge and blower start together, or a GFCI outlet on the generator is reacting to the house wiring. Shed other loads first, then use the generator's non-GFCI twist-lock outlet through the proper inlet. Our <a href="/generator-runs-but-no-power/">generator runs but no power</a> guide covers other faults. Before the first cold snap, top it up with the right <a href="/best-generator-oil/">generator oil</a> for winter starts, and pair it with a properly rated <a href="/best-generator-extension-cord/">generator cord</a> for the inlet box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding the Panel Through a Dryer Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord energizes the utility lines and the bare prongs of the cord, and it can kill a line worker down the street. It also leaves your furnace without a reliable neutral bond. Pay for an inlet box with an interlock kit or a transfer switch. It costs less than one service call on a fried furnace board, and it is the only legal way to do it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Cheap Open-Frame Generator for a Modern Furnace</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A conventional generator with high harmonic distortion may run an old PSC blower, but the circuit board and ECM motor in a high-efficiency furnace want clean power. Owners commonly report error codes and hunting blowers on dirty power. Spending a little more on an inverter rated under 3% THD protects the most expensive electrical part of your heating system.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the Furnace Alone, Then Adding the Fridge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator that handles the furnace with a few hundred watts to spare will trip when the refrigerator compressor and the blower start at the same moment. Add up everything you plan to run, include each motor's starting surge, and leave at least 20% headroom. That margin is also what keeps an inverter quiet and fuel-efficient.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Parking the Generator Next to the Furnace Intake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                High-efficiency furnaces pull combustion air through a sidewall pipe. A generator placed near it can feed exhaust straight into the furnace and the house. Keep the generator at least 20 feet from the house, exhaust pointed away from every window, door and vent, and confirm where both furnace pipes exit before you choose a spot.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Testing the Setup for the First Time During the Storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A generator that sat all summer with old gasoline, a dead start battery or a cord that turns out to be too short is a problem you want to discover in October. Run the generator under load before winter, switch the furnace over through the transfer switch once to confirm it lights and stays lit, and stock fuel then.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best generator for a furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes the Honda EU3000iS is the best generator for a furnace on a dedicated circuit: clean inverter power, electric start and an overnight tank. If you have a 240V inlet box, the WEN DF680iX adds an L14-30R outlet and dual fuel. For the furnace plus most of the house, the Westinghouse iGen12000DFc is the clean-power choice with 50A and 30A outlets.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need to run my furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most gas furnaces draw 600 to 1,200 running watts, with a blower start-up surge two to three times the blower's running draw. A 2,500 to 3,000-watt inverter runs the furnace alone. If you also want a refrigerator, lights and internet, choose 3,500 to 5,000 running watts. Check the furnace nameplate amps and multiply by 120 for a precise figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my furnace into a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not directly. Furnaces are hardwired, so the safe options are a single-circuit furnace transfer switch, an inlet box with an interlock kit, or a manual transfer switch, all installed by a licensed electrician. Some installers fit a plug and receptacle at the furnace for this purpose, but that must meet local code. Never backfeed the house through a dryer or wall outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need an inverter generator for a gas furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For any furnace with a circuit board or an ECM variable-speed blower, which covers nearly every furnace sold in the last twenty years, an inverter is strongly recommended. Inverters are typically rated under 3% THD, close to grid power. Distorted power from some conventional generators can cause blower hunting, error codes and board damage that costs more than the price difference.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my furnace shut off when running on a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because the control board cannot sense the flame. Flame sensing needs a neutral-to-ground reference, and a generator with a floating neutral feeding a furnace through a cord often lacks one. Connecting through a transfer switch or interlock tied to your bonded main panel usually solves it. Reversed polarity in an adapter is the second most common cause.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long can a generator run a furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                As long as you keep it fueled and the oil fresh. A mid-size inverter running a furnace and refrigerator typically sits around 25% load, where the Honda EU3000iS is rated for up to 19.6 hours per tank and the Champion 4500 for up to 21 hours on propane. Check the oil every day or so during a long outage, as the manual specifies.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run a furnace?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for a while. A gas furnace drawing 500 to 800 watts while the blower runs, cycling about half the time, uses several kilowatt-hours a day in cold weather. A 2kWh station covers part of a night. Stations are useful for short outages or as a bridge, but a fueled generator is still the practical choice for multi-day winter outages.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

