        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Keeping a furnace running in an outage is less about wattage than people expect. Almost any 3,000-watt inverter has the power. What decides success is clean power for the control board and blower, a safe connection through an inlet and a transfer switch or interlock, and a generator placed well away from the house and the furnace's intake pipe.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For a dedicated furnace circuit, the Honda EU3000iS is the easiest recommendation. For a standard 240V inlet box, the WEN DF680iX is the value pick and the Honda EU7000iS the premium one. If you want the whole house, the Westinghouse iGen12000DFc does it on clean power. Whichever you choose, set it up and test it before the first storm, not during it.
          </p>
